  "answer_text": "It looks like you're referring to a **GMAT Sentence Correction** question! Here is a full breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcdd GMAT Sentence Correction Question\n\n**Full Sentence:**\n\"Unable to build nests or care for their young, a female cowbird lays up to 40 eggs a year in the nests of other birds, including warblers, vireos, flycatchers, and thrushes.\"\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd22 Answer Choices\n\n\n- **A.** a female cowbird lays up to 40 eggs a year in the nests of other birds, including\n- **B.** a female cowbird will use the nests of other birds to lay up to 40 eggs a year, including those of\n- **C.** female cowbirds use the nests of other birds to lay up to 40 eggs a year, including those of\n- **D.** female cowbirds lay up to 40 eggs a year in the nests of other birds, including\n- **E.** up to 40 eggs a year are laid by female cowbirds in the nests of other birds, including\n\n\n\u2705 **Official Answer: D**\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcd6 Explanation\n\n### \u274c Eliminating A & B:\nIn A and B, **\"their\"** (plural) does not agree with **\"a female cowbird\"** (singular). Notice that the modifier says \"care for **their** young,\" so it needs the **plural** form of cowbird.\n\n### \u274c Eliminating C:\nIn C, \"female cowbirds...lay up to 40 eggs a year, **including those of** warblers\" \u2014 here, **\"those\"** seems to refer to *eggs*, the nearest preceding plural noun \u2014 yielding the meaning: \"Female cowbirds lay up to 40 eggs a year, *including the eggs of warblers*.\" This distorts the intended meaning.\n\n### \u274c Eliminating E:\nE is out due to **incorrect modification**. The passive construction moves \"female cowbirds\" away from the opening modifier, creating a dangling modifier error.\n\n### \u2705 Why D is Correct:\nThe first major problem is the modifier **\"Unable to build nests or care for their young\"** \u2014 this modifier must be followed by **'female cowbirds'**. Option D correctly places **\"female cowbirds\"** immediately after the introductory modifier, satisfies the plural agreement with \"their,\" and clearly conveys that cowbirds lay eggs *in the nests of other birds* (not the eggs *of* other birds).\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udddd\ufe0f Key Grammar Concepts Tested:\n| Concept | Issue |\n|---|---|\n| **Dangling/Misplaced Modifier** | The opening phrase must modify the correct subject |\n| **Pronoun-Antecedent Agreement** | \"Their\" requires a plural antecedent |\n| **Ambiguous Pronoun Reference** | \"Those\" in C creates meaning confusion |\n| **Passive vs. Active Voice** | E's passive structure causes a modifier error |",
